  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Improved decent employment opportunities through the development of employment programmes and policies in Tonga</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The ILO has been supporting the development of Tonga&apos;s first-ever National Employment Policy (NEP) a coherent, comprehensive and integrated framework that promotes full and productive employment and decent work for all, in line with the SDGs. The NEP mainstreams employment in national policy-making and strives to achieve a dynamic and inclusive labour market by promoting job creation, enhancing skills development, and ensuring that employment opportunities are available and accessible to all. The NEP draft has been finalized on the basis of an employment situation analysis as well as broad-based consultations and engagement with key stakeholders, including multiple government agencies, employers&apos; and workers&apos; organizations, and other relevant stakeholders. This collaborative and inclusive approach ensures that the NEP reflects stakeholders&apos; views, ensuring their support and commitment. By addressing several relevant areas, the ILO aims to create a dynamic and inclusive labour market that benefits everyone in Tonga. The Government plans to adopt the NEP in the fourth quarter of 2025 and then move forward with its implementation, following the action plan and implementation mechanisms that form part of the NEP. The work has been supported by the ILO-led joint programme &quot;Advancing the SDGs by improving livelihoods and resilience via economic diversification and digital transformation&quot;, funded by the Joint SDG Fund. The ILO is also supporting the development of a National Apprenticeship Policy (NAP) , which aims to create a mechanism for collaboration and coordination among various ministries in the area of apprenticeships. It will include a concrete plan to promote apprenticeships as a new form of training across different sectors and occupations. The plan will also identify target sectors and occupations and gather input on regulations and financing.</narrative>
  </description>
